%% gai_boot.glp --- the app of GAI, accountable AI agents, on three smartphones (GSG paper, Sections 2 and 6, "The Grassroots Super-App" and "Mini-Apps on the Prototype"), as gsnp_boot.glp runs GSNP's app on five: under the boot harness, one isolate per agent, each with its own key pair, running the social graph of programs/social/graph/core as its super-app and hosting GAI's mini-app (programs/apps/gai).  An agent of GAI's paper is "a person or an AI agent, with its machine" (Section 3.2), so alf, alice's AI agent, has a smartphone of its own, on which its program takes the AI agent's acts.  The program is graph/core, named as :boot's second argument, and this file is loaded on top of it, with the boot clause and the persons; the smartphone, gai_phone/4, is graph/core's (core/gai_phone.glp), started by core's agent/2 on the person's first act (IGLP 0e1c9e0, Implementation Notes, "Boot"):
%%   :artefact programs/apps/gai programs/social/graph/core
%%   :boot programs/apps/gai_boot.glp programs/social/graph/core
%%
%% The agents are those of the play of programs/apps/gai, alice, alf and bob (plays.glp there).  Each trusts the compiler's key, reads the certified artefact gai.glpw from graph/core with load_file/2 and installs it, with one end of a person channel.  alice connects to alf; once connected, alf connects to bob, and bob to alice; each pair befriends in the super-app's social graph across the isolates; once connected to the next, each invites them to the mini-app, and the invitee, having consented in advance, joins it, each handshake opening a root channel between the two executions.  Over those three conversations the mini-app runs GAI's contract, as play_agents of programs/apps/gai runs it: alice and bob establish their identities (GKK:1) and come to know each other (GKK:2); alice signs the code of alf and holds it (GAI:1), and enters her AI agent alf with it, attesting that alf runs it (GnW:1, GAI:1, GAI:2); alice, its principal, proposes alf's meeting with bob, her machine filling the attestation from her record, alf and bob asked, bob recording alf, its principal and its attestation (GnW:2, GAI:2), and then its befriending him, alf and bob asked (GnW:2); bob and alice become friends (GSG:1); alice retires alf (GAI:4); and bob ends his friendship with alf (GSG:2).  The item of the paper's example is GSNP's, the full platform (gsnp_boot.glp).  The other end of each person channel is the mini-app's own scripted person (programs/apps/gai/person.glp), alf's the AI agent's program, activated from the module value under its role; its screen goes to the agent, one line each, and so does what the super-app told the agent of the friendships in its own social graph, sg(...).
%%
%% The boot settles, as gsnp_boot.glp's does: the boot harness reduces each event until the program is quiescent, as the device runtime does (IGLP, Implementation Notes: "On each event the agent binds what the event delivers, reduces until quiescent"), and after each event the platform's agent takes its own transactions until none holds and then waits for the next (vGLP, Section 5.4, "Triggers").  Section APPS of the suite runs this file and holds each agent's lines, in order.

procedure boot.
boot :-
    agent(ch(_?, _), ch(_?, _))@alice,
    say(alice, alice, [bob, alf], ch(_?, _))@alice,
    agent(ch(_?, _), ch(_?, _))@alf,
    say(alf, alf, [alice, bob], ch(_?, _))@alf,
    agent(ch(_?, _), ch(_?, _))@bob,
    say(bob, bob, [alf, alice], ch(_?, _))@bob.

%% Every agent's initial goal is agent(ch(_?,_),ch(_?,_)), the person channel first and the network channel second (GLP-Spec maglp.tex, Definition "Multiagent GLP"), which the program, graph/core, answers: a boot defines no agent/2, and the person goal's first act names the entry the program starts (IGLP 0e1c9e0, Implementation Notes, "Boot").  The person scripted at each agent, say/4, run on its person channel in the person's place, tells the agent the entry, gai_phone/4 (graph/core/gai_phone.glp), with the person's part in the script, their own key and the keys of the peers they know, its ground arguments, the readable names being the atoms that stand for the keys in a simulation ("An agent learns the keys of its peers from its person"; Remark "Agent Names"), and reads nothing.
Role     ::= alice ; alf ; bob.
SayAct ::= gai_phone(Role, Key, List(Key)).
SayIn  ::= [SayAct | SayEnd].
SayEnd ::= [].

exported procedure say(Role?, Key?, List(Key)?, Channel(Stream(Constant), SayIn)?).
say(R, Me, Ps, ch(_, [gai_phone(R?, Me?, Ps?)])).
